"The prognosis following radical prostatectomy is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins, absence of seminal vesicle invasion, and lack of lymphovascular invasion. The pathological findings showed a Gleason 4+3 (ISUP grade group 3) in the prostatectomy specimen, and the biopsy showed a progression from Gleason 0+0 to 3+4 over time. Countervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 9.5 and the absence of lymph node removal, which widens uncertainty due to missing nodal sampling. The difference between biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, though the surgical pathology was Gleason 4+3. Postoperative PSA and its trajectory are critical unknowns that would significantly revise this assessment. Missing nodal sampling specifically increases the uncertainty regarding nodal status. The current horizon suggests a follow-up period of approximately 60 months without an event. The fixed estimated horizon is 60.33 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 39.21–81.44 months; its coverage has not been validated."
